{"signature": "d7c5b8cb65f30701c842c5bdd0ef8c6c783633bc9c054e1d95d1fa22d880fc66", "result": {"checks": {"check": {"scope": {"id": "check", "service": "gslides", "file": "data.json", "pointer": "/slides_presentations", "field": "presentation_id", "values": ["f_02ebfaf11d3f622b57e9bb", "f_ffd7fdb72db51775ff6d67"]}, "matches": [{"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/gslides/data.json", "pointer": "/slides_presentations/1", "field": "presentation_id", "value": "f_02ebfaf11d3f622b57e9bb", "record": {"presentation_id": "f_02ebfaf11d3f622b57e9bb", "title": "Domino Delights Design Post Mortem", "owner_email": "julia.lawson@harmonygames.co", "shared_with_emails": ["brian.foster@harmonygames.co"], "visibility": "private", "pages": [{"object_id": "slide_d343910f6f6e", "index": 0, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_5230587fdbd5", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Why developers\nno follow doc?", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_a9c892d6f71e", "index": 1, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_52e6fdea2f60", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "What we asked them?\n\n2\n\nDo you think the design documents are\nadequate?\n01\nIf you could change one thing about the\n02 documents what would that be?\nIf you were making these documents, what\n03 would you do differently.\n04\n\nDo you have any general feedback for the\ndesign team? What could we improve or do\nbetter?\nHow do you think the communication between\n\n05\n\nmultiple engineers and the designer be?\n\nWhat's your general workflow and do you think it\n06 should be standardized in any way?\n07\n\nWhy do you think delays happen?", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_bee64ce651fb", "index": 2, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_185c2d13beb4", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "OVERVIEW\n\n3\n\nThe engineering team are\noverall very happy with us.\nThey find the design team\nkind, helpful and\ncommunicative.\nDOCUMENT\n\nCOMMUNICATION\n\nIMPROVEMENT\n\nAll engineers find the\ndocuments to be sufficient and\nthe language understandable.\nBut they want more visual\ninstructions.\n\nThey admit to not always\nfollowing the channels because\nthey find it noisy. They want\nchannels to have general\ndiscussion and solve everything\nelse in smaller groups or 1-1.\n\nThey want us to spend more\ntime writing and designing so\nthat we can detect edger cases\nearly on.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_e506fd4ad350", "index": 3, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_8685c75f9adc", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "DELAYS THOUGH\n\n4\n\nthink things are\n\nEngineers\n\nmoving quite fast!\nASSET BANK\nThey appreciate SAMUEL?s UI\n\nimplementation a lot! They\nwant to spend less time\nimporting and more time\ncoding.\n\nGOOD DESIGN PILLARS\nThey want better architecture\nstarting from the document.\nWhen specs are not tied to a\n[USERNAME_4231],\n\nare a lot of\n\nIDs and classes and not\neveryone knows they exist.\n\nCONSTANT FEEDBACK AND DESIGN\nSTABILITY\n\nThey want things to be designed once and not iterated later. They want\nthe finalized design to be checked every time There\n\nis progress on a\n\nbranch/video/build and give constant feedback that things are going okay.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_9667e6738819", "index": 4, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_559b7758423f", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "ACTION ITEMS\nMore visuals and more\naccountability.\n1. ADD WIREFRAMES TO FLOW\n2. ASK Developer\n\nTO VET THE\n\nDOCUMENT AND HOLD\nACCOUNTABLE\n3. DIVIDE SUBTASKS, ASK FOR\nPROVESS AND CHECK DAILY\n4. TRY CREATE NON-PARALLEL BACK\nEND work\n\n(LOL)\n\n5", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_ddcce7132168", "index": 5, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_70c4c52f72fb", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Thank You\nWe have arrived at the part\nwhere we all understand what\u2019s\ngoing on but engage\n\nin\n\nconversation to be polite.\n\n6", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}], "drive_file_id": "f_02ebfaf11d3f622b57e9bb", "revision_id": "93dbb60710", "created_time": "2025-03-25T15:55:42Z", "modified_time": "2025-03-25T16:31:51Z"}}, {"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/gslides/data.json", "pointer": "/slides_presentations/163", "field": "presentation_id", "value": "f_ffd7fdb72db51775ff6d67", "record": {"presentation_id": "f_ffd7fdb72db51775ff6d67", "title": "ZM Level Design Guide", "owner_email": "julia.lawson@harmonygames.co", "shared_with_emails": [], "visibility": "domain", "pages": [{"object_id": "slide_8124cbdb6d57", "index": 0, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_eea47285eb31", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "\u25cf\n\ncore design principles are shown on\n\n\u25cf\n\nThe first 100 levels are to teach the player\nall the core gameplay values and strategies\nthey can form.\nMake levels that either rewards mastery or\nspeed. Make sure to rotate between them\nsufficiently for gameplay variety.\nMonochromatic and texture heavy levels\nshould reward speed, whereas levels where\neither an item or zombie is being introduced\nshould reward mastery instead should be a\n\u201cno brainer\u201d level.\nThe approaches can be combined after level\n100.\n\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\n\u25cf\n\u2756\n\u2756\n\u2756\n\u25cf\n\nDon\u2019t match approaches back to back too\nmuch.\nDon\u2019t match more than 2 until level 200\nMatch 3 sparingly after level 200.\nThe levels solution should be obvious in the\nfirst glance. That\u2019s not to say levels should\nbe easy, it just means, on the first look, the\nstrategy should be obvious, especially for\nthe first 100 levels. We\u2019ll confuse players\nlater on after d7 levels by introducing nonobvious levels or trick strategy levels so in\nearly levels, please avoid these kind of\ntricks.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_9b1cbc1302b7", "index": 1, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_cae3c86f2506", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "How To Use\nColors?\n\nWe have 4 types of color levels. Levels should try to\nadhere to these types or use lighter monster track if\nusing a combination of multiple.\n\nMonochrome\n\u25cf\n\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nWhen making monochrome levels, use objects of the same color that has\nat least one other color or detail that\u2019s in another color. Pure color items\nare okay, but they shouldn\u2019t exceed 2-3 and should be still visually\ndistinguishable via shape or texture.\nUse monochrome levels sparingly and make sure to rotate between colors.\nWhen making monochromatic levels make sure you choose a middle\nsaturation unless some textures make it okay to go higher or lower.\nPlayers take a longer time to solve a monochrome level so make sure to\ncompensate with a more forgiving monster track.\nTry to follow with a more forgiving level after a monochromatic level. Aim\nto follow up with a level that has items distinguishable through shape and\ntexture (e.g. like a ball level). At the very least, try to change up the\ngameplay style focus.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_505edb1b6cd5", "index": 2, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_fa294b1790df", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Complementary Color\nUsage\n\u25cf\n\nWhen players look at a level made out of complementary colors, they will perceive it as a\nwhole. Use this to to the levels advantage to steer players towards a certain color via texture\n\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\n\u25cf\n\nand saturation.\nThis type of level will get hard in the middle. So make sure your monster track intensifies or\ndecrease monster track difficulty based on the level difficulty you want. Levels after 70ish are\neligible to support an increase in the middle rhythm in a complementary color level.\nDistribution of colors doesn\u2019t really matter. If you have equal amount of the colors, player will\ngravitate towards texture and saturation. Same is true if one color is less dominant, therefore\nlooking more prominent on top of it\u2019s complementary counterpart. Use forced tops and\nbottoms if you are concerned about preventing abuse.\nGood practice is to use items and details of various sizes for each color. Otherwise the level\nwill be unfairly difficult and luck will play too much role. If your goal is to create more rhythm\ntowards the end, you can use similar items but I recommend you force them bottom to create\na nice gameplay shift and layer\n\n.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_8974b3d14811", "index": 3, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_2f2e52263d2c", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Analogue\nLevels\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nAnalogue levels are a great alternative to monochrome levels and allow for more flexibility in\nitem selection.\nAnalogue levels items should be more or less split equally between colors. If There\n\nis an\n\nimbalance, unlike the complimentary levels, the level will always be a lot more easier.\nAnalogue levels are great for using the same item of different colors.\nIf you go for three analogue colors, the third color, either the left one or the right one needs\nto become an accent color and have less objects in that color. If you want accent items\nwithout adding another color, go for texture accents.\nNeutral colors can always be used as an alternative to a third analogue.\n\nTexture\naccent\n\nExample of third\nanalogue being an\naccent", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_486accbd66a2", "index": 4, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_44fedd1ec3f5", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Triad and Tetrad\nLevels\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nTriad and Tetrad levels should be considered when a noisy level is required for variety.\nThey can use any triad and tetrad combination and include a neutral, but sparingly.\nNeutrals shouldn\u2019t include items the same color as the triad or the tetrad. So if a red shuriken\nis present, a gray shuriken shouldn\u2019t be in the level. So it should be avoided in general, but\nokay to use sparingly.\nMake sure to use textures in such levels.\nUse these levels if you want to create levels with similar size items.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_3c9f9f518693", "index": 5, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_27c517cb08cd", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Experimental\nLevels\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nHere is an example of an experimental level. It combines a monochrome level with a texture\nchallenge.\nFeel free to throw this kind of level in the funnel to see player reaction for readiness for\nmechanic combination etc.\nIf you have this type of experimental level, make sure it falls in the middle of well learned\npassive skills. For example make a level like this after introducing monochromatic levels and\ntexture challenge levels and There\n\nmuscle in both mechanics.\n\nare enough of them for the players to have gained the", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_f8ebde712a7e", "index": 6, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_3390890778fa", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "The Role of Rhythm in Level Making\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nA good level should have a rhythm unique to that level. And a good curve should include variety of level rhythms.\nFor casual games, avoid using rhythm curves that start fast and slow down over time. Unless you catch a fun one, start slow\nand build into it.\nThe core principle of using rhythm in level making is to make sure the player falls into repetitive motion and gets zoned\nout/relaxed while engaging with the content. So as long as this is achieved, your rhythm is working.\nSince we\u2019re working with CPM, you can easily test your methods with the level design bot.\nYou can use rhythm via objects or the monsters, both will work\n\n.\n\nItem Rhythm\n\n\u25cf\n\nA good level should have a rhythm unique to that\nlevel. And a good curve should include variety of\nlevel rhythms.\n\nMonster Rhythm\n\u25cf\n\nA good level should have a rhythm unique to that\nlevel. And a good curve should include variety of\nlevel rhythms.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_3ce9bbf32d1c", "index": 7, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_0f581d17d269", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "Core Progression Principles\n\u25cf\n\nThe main principle of the level progression is subjecting players to a certain CPM, and failing\nthem if they don\u2019t match the speed required.\nThis requirement begins at very low CPMs until the core game learning curve levels are passed and then\nprogressively becomes harder, capping at a CPM that will be determined by the data later but projected\nto land at 100-120 CPM as a plateau.\nAs a secondary target, the level durations should also curve and increase over time, eventually reaching\nthe cap of 90-120 seconds based on the objects of a level.\n\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nOther Considerations\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\u25cf\n\nThe levels should have zombies reaching the cannon at the target CPM. The logic being, if the players\n\nfalls short of the CPM the zombies would pile at the cannon and create urgency.\nTry to put zombies at exact times to make sure to achieve this effect, especially in levels that have area\naffect items to ensure easier balance.\nLevel balance target is for players to finish a level without cards, winstreaks, or boosters at\nthe target CPM.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}, {"object_id": "slide_a693a1d83078", "index": 8, "layout": "BLANK", "elements": [{"object_id": "el_cdb9214a15f4", "kind": "TEXT_BOX", "text": "CPM Targets\n1-30 \u2192 50 CPM\n30-75 \u2192 60CPM\n75-100 ? 70 CPM\n\n100-150 \u2192 75 CPM (will change according to data) = Almost 2 minutes to clear 48 items\n150 - onward \u2192 80-90 CPM (will change according to data)\n\nDuration\nTargets\n\nLevels 1-5 \u2192 15 seconds\nLevels 5-10 \u2192 25 seconds\nLevels 10-15 \u2192 35 seconds\nLevels 25-40 \u2192 45 seconds\nLevels 40-75 \u2192 60 seconds\nLevels 75-100 \u2192 75 seconds\nLevels 100-150 \u2192 90 seconds\nLevels 150-onward \u2192 90-120 seconds.\n\nItem Targets\n3 color scheme max\n\nwith occasional\n\nmulticolor levels or 4 for item intro\nlevels.\n4-5 quantity (6 max)\n5-6 unique items (6 max)\nAfter level 150 we\u2019ll bump this by\n1, and CPM by 5 until the\nretention data endures the\nincreased difficulty.", "placeholder_type": null, "x_emu": 0, "y_emu": 0, "width_emu": 9144000, "height_emu": 5143500}]}], "drive_file_id": "f_ffd7fdb72db51775ff6d67", "revision_id": "fbf725fb03", "created_time": "2025-09-09T14:37:31Z", "modified_time": "2025-09-11T17:04:12Z"}}], "records_checked": 164, "files_selected": 1, "files_completed": 1, "scan_complete": true, "errors": []}}, "files_read_this_call": 2, "sources": [{"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/gslides/data.json", "manifest_sha256": "c3782541395e178187d732eb429a7e69926244856147a8c4f5091b0f9e7a7426", "freshness": "f234fec647af2dbc77f101b0eb50bf1c0f7ba155b2b960031fe8340010fbbb1f"}], "scan_complete": true, "persona_filtered": false, "backend": "grouped-record-scan", "scope_note": "Exact direct fields in selected arrays. No SQL is applied. Absence/uniqueness is supported only within complete declared scopes. Manifest hashes are declared provenance, not fresh full-content hashes."}, "checksum": "5722015dc62d4f8567a129b6ffb45dc123fbf99ee759bab11f8f201f755ed28a"}